1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:02,946
Pulau Bukom: The Kerosene Island That

2
00:00:03,026 --> 00:00:05,725
Became Singapore's First Refinery

3
00:00:05,725 --> 00:00:09,655
From the Sentosa waterfront,
across a mile of anchorage,

4
00:00:09,735 --> 00:00:12,233
stands an island of tanks and towers

5
00:00:12,313 --> 00:00:15,750
that almost no Singaporean
has ever set foot on.

6
00:00:15,750 --> 00:00:20,367
Pulau Bukom is closed to the
public, reached by a workers' ferry,

7
00:00:20,447 --> 00:00:23,546
and it has been handling
oil longer than any

8
00:00:23,626 --> 00:00:27,737
other place in Singapore —
since 1892, when what its tanks

9
00:00:27,817 --> 00:00:31,575
held was not petrol for
cars but kerosene for lamps.

10
00:00:31,575 --> 00:00:36,435
It was a storage island first, then
a town of five thousand people,

11
00:00:36,515 --> 00:00:38,875
then Singapore's first refinery.

12
00:00:38,875 --> 00:00:42,385
This is the story of the
island that oil built,

13
00:00:42,465 --> 00:00:44,375
emptied, and built again.

14
00:00:44,375 --> 00:00:48,081
Bukom lies about five and
a half kilometres southwest

15
00:00:48,161 --> 00:00:51,875
of the mainland, in the
cluster of southern islands.

16
00:00:51,875 --> 00:00:55,540
Before oil, it was a mangrove
island known to passing

17
00:00:55,620 --> 00:00:58,438
ships as a place to take on fresh water —

18
00:00:58,518 --> 00:01:02,475
the Dutch called it
Zoetwater-eiland, Freshwater Island.

19
00:01:02,475 --> 00:01:07,055
Its Malay name is usually traced
to rangkek bukom, a cone shell wide

20
00:01:07,135 --> 00:01:11,373
at one end and tapering to a
point, which the island's original

21
00:01:11,453 --> 00:01:16,719
shape was said to resemble; another old
account derives it instead from hukum,

22
00:01:16,799 --> 00:01:20,500
judgement, for a ruler said
to have tried cases there.

23
00:01:20,500 --> 00:01:23,866
In the nineteenth century
the island's owner

24
00:01:23,946 --> 00:01:27,000
was Captain Giovanni Gaggino, a trader.

25
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:29,609
What changed its life was a

26
00:01:29,689 --> 00:01:32,975
decision taken in London in 1891.

27
00:01:32,975 --> 00:01:38,253
In 1891 the London firm M. Samuel
& Co. chose Singapore as its base

28
00:01:38,333 --> 00:01:44,012
for importing Russian kerosene in
bulk and distributing it across Asia —

29
00:01:44,092 --> 00:01:49,050
lamp oil, in the age before
electric light reached most homes.

30
00:01:49,050 --> 00:01:52,577
Samuel appointed the
local agency house Syme

31
00:01:52,657 --> 00:01:56,100
& Co. to establish a petroleum tank depot.

32
00:01:56,100 --> 00:02:01,019
The government refused to allow
bulk petroleum storage in town,

33
00:02:01,099 --> 00:02:06,495
so the depot went offshore: in
September 1891 Syme & Co. bought eight

34
00:02:06,575 --> 00:02:12,050
hectares on Bukom from Captain
Gaggino for $3,000 and began building.

35
00:02:12,050 --> 00:02:18,289
The depot cost over $60,000, with
tank capacity of 4,500 tonnes,

36
00:02:18,369 --> 00:02:21,825
a wharf and ship-to-tank pipelines.

37
00:02:21,825 --> 00:02:25,149
It opened on the 16th of September 1892,

38
00:02:25,229 --> 00:02:29,825
when the first shipment
arrived aboard the S.S. Murex.

39
00:02:29,825 --> 00:02:34,299
The National Library Board calls
it the first depot of its kind

40
00:02:34,379 --> 00:02:38,347
in the East, and Singapore's
first oil storage facility;

41
00:02:38,427 --> 00:02:43,125
by 1902, Bukom had become the oil
supply centre for the Far East.

42
00:02:43,125 --> 00:02:45,771
The kerosene island was, in effect,

43
00:02:45,851 --> 00:02:49,200
the founding site of
Singapore's oil trade.

44
00:02:49,200 --> 00:02:53,686
The Samuel business became, in
1897, a separate company called

45
00:02:53,766 --> 00:02:58,620
Shell Transport and Trading, and
in 1907 it merged with Royal Dutch

46
00:02:58,700 --> 00:03:03,407
Petroleum to form Royal Dutch
Shell — so the island's tenant grew

47
00:03:03,487 --> 00:03:08,200
into one of the largest companies
on earth without ever leaving.

48
00:03:08,200 --> 00:03:11,563
The connection ran deep
into local business life:

49
00:03:11,643 --> 00:03:16,553
the Lee family's firm, Lee Chim Tuan,
handled stevedoring and materials

50
00:03:16,633 --> 00:03:20,067
for Shell at Bukom from
the industry's early days,

51
00:03:20,147 --> 00:03:24,494
and when Lee Chim Tuan died in
1955, the flag at Shell's signal

52
00:03:24,574 --> 00:03:28,650
station on the island was
lowered to half-mast in respect.

53
00:03:28,650 --> 00:03:31,901
By 1942 the depot had grown to sixty

54
00:03:31,981 --> 00:03:34,850
storage tanks and five wharves.

55
00:03:34,850 --> 00:03:39,209
As the Japanese advanced down the
peninsula, the British torched

56
00:03:39,289 --> 00:03:44,075
forty-two of the tanks to keep the
oil from falling into enemy hands.

57
00:03:44,075 --> 00:03:48,918
During the Occupation that followed,
Bukom served as a prisoner-of-war camp:

58
00:03:48,998 --> 00:03:52,480
some three hundred Australian
and New Zealand prisoners

59
00:03:52,560 --> 00:03:56,900
were put to work salvaging the
wrecked plant they had watched burn.

60
00:03:56,900 --> 00:04:01,510
After the war, Shell's employees
repaired the installation,

61
00:04:01,591 --> 00:04:04,850
and by 1950 it was back in working order.

62
00:04:04,850 --> 00:04:10,000
What the post-war island became was
not just a tank farm but a town.

63
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:15,159
In the early 1960s as many as five
thousand people lived on Bukom,

64
00:04:15,239 --> 00:04:19,050
most of them Shell employees
and their families.

65
00:04:19,050 --> 00:04:24,082
The island had housing, two markets,
chapels and a mosque, a Hindu temple,

66
00:04:24,162 --> 00:04:28,100
two schools, a clinic, four
clubs and an open-air cinema.

67
00:04:28,100 --> 00:04:32,313
On Bukom Besar stood the company's
quarters — the rumah putih,

68
00:04:32,393 --> 00:04:36,330
the white houses, for staff
families, and the rumah merah,

69
00:04:36,410 --> 00:04:41,315
the red houses, for officers, engineers
and doctors — while the islander

70
00:04:41,395 --> 00:04:45,550
kampong stood on Bukom Kechil,
the smaller island beside it.

71
00:04:45,550 --> 00:04:49,025
One islander's story
carries the whole arc.

72
00:04:49,025 --> 00:04:53,678
Ahmadi bin Mukasan, known as
Madi, was born and bred on Bukom;

73
00:04:53,758 --> 00:04:58,950
his father had come from Banyumas
in Java and found work with Shell.

74
00:04:58,950 --> 00:05:03,869
In 1961 the family was resettled
to a kampong house in Kaki Bukit —

75
00:05:03,949 --> 00:05:08,645
"We didn't want to leave initially,"
he recalled — but Madi came

76
00:05:08,725 --> 00:05:13,793
back to the island to work, and
lived there again as a Shell fireman,

77
00:05:13,873 --> 00:05:18,350
"Fireman 18", facing a major
refinery fire in February 1989.

78
00:05:18,350 --> 00:05:22,750
The family finally left
Bukom in the 1990s.

79
00:05:22,750 --> 00:05:27,440
By 1959 Bukom was already the
biggest centre in Southeast

80
00:05:27,520 --> 00:05:32,625
Asia for the storage, blending,
packing and bunkering of oil.

81
00:05:32,625 --> 00:05:37,050
That December, Shell announced
something larger: a refinery.

82
00:05:37,050 --> 00:05:41,347
The agreement with the Singapore
government was signed on

83
00:05:41,427 --> 00:05:45,341
the 1st of March 1960,
construction began that June,

84
00:05:45,421 --> 00:05:50,332
and on the 26th of July 1961 the
Finance Minister, Goh Keng Swee,

85
00:05:50,412 --> 00:05:54,175
officially opened Singapore's
first oil refinery.

86
00:05:54,175 --> 00:05:57,668
Shell received the government's
Pioneer Certificate

87
00:05:57,748 --> 00:06:01,102
No. 1 for it — the first
award of the scheme that

88
00:06:01,182 --> 00:06:04,475
would underwrite Singapore's
industrialisation.

89
00:06:04,475 --> 00:06:07,880
The plant cost $30
million and could process

90
00:06:07,960 --> 00:06:11,525
about twenty thousand
barrels of crude a day.

91
00:06:11,525 --> 00:06:15,305
Over the next two decades
four more refineries were

92
00:06:15,385 --> 00:06:19,013
added on the island, and
by 1980 Bukom's capacity

93
00:06:19,093 --> 00:06:22,575
had reached twenty-five
million tonnes a year.

94
00:06:22,575 --> 00:06:25,750
Growth consumed the island it had made.

95
00:06:25,750 --> 00:06:28,415
As operations spread onto Bukom Kechil,

96
00:06:28,495 --> 00:06:33,483
the inhabitants there — some two
thousand people — were resettled around

97
00:06:33,563 --> 00:06:37,707
1970 to make way for crude oil
storage; where they all went,

98
00:06:37,787 --> 00:06:41,719
the record does not say, and
the named moves that survive

99
00:06:41,799 --> 00:06:45,600
are individual ones, like
Madi's family to Kaki Bukit.

100
00:06:45,600 --> 00:06:50,050
A bridge joined the two Bukoms in 1969.

101
00:06:50,050 --> 00:06:54,790
Hills on both islands were levelled
for tank space in the 1970s,

102
00:06:54,870 --> 00:06:58,179
and the reef of Pulau
Ular was reclaimed into

103
00:06:58,259 --> 00:07:02,250
a thirty-hectare island and
linked by another bridge.

104
00:07:02,250 --> 00:07:07,067
By 1970 only twelve hundred
people still lived on Bukom Besar,

105
00:07:07,147 --> 00:07:12,675
and most workers were already what
they remain today: ferry commuters.

106
00:07:12,675 --> 00:07:17,450
The last of the island families
held on remarkably late.

107
00:07:17,450 --> 00:07:22,229
Leng Ng Tai, who joined Shell
in 1969, lived on Bukom with his

108
00:07:22,309 --> 00:07:27,089
family into the 1990s — by then
twenty-eight families remained

109
00:07:27,169 --> 00:07:31,950
in two housing blocks, alongside
firemen and police families.

110
00:07:31,950 --> 00:07:33,654
Doors were never locked;

111
00:07:33,734 --> 00:07:37,450
a dance instructor was
ferried in every Wednesday.

112
00:07:37,450 --> 00:07:42,650
The Lengs moved to a West
Coast terrace house in 1997.

113
00:07:42,650 --> 00:07:45,332
His son Benny works for the same company,

114
00:07:45,412 --> 00:07:49,375
and father and son still make
the journey to Bukom together:

115
00:07:49,455 --> 00:07:52,150
car to the jetty, then the ferry across.

116
00:07:52,150 --> 00:07:56,716
On the 31st of January 1974,
Bukom became the scene of

117
00:07:56,796 --> 00:08:01,700
Singapore's first encounter
with international terrorism.

118
00:08:01,700 --> 00:08:05,258
Four armed men — two from
the Japanese Red Army and

119
00:08:05,338 --> 00:08:09,539
two from the Popular Front for
the Liberation of Palestine —

120
00:08:09,619 --> 00:08:11,679
attacked the refinery complex,

121
00:08:11,759 --> 00:08:16,325
detonating explosives at three
oil tanks and setting one ablaze.

122
00:08:16,325 --> 00:08:19,232
The fire was quickly put out and

123
00:08:19,312 --> 00:08:22,300
the damage came to some $45,000.

124
00:08:22,300 --> 00:08:26,223
The attackers then hijacked
the ferryboat Laju at the Bukom

125
00:08:26,303 --> 00:08:30,633
jetty and held its crew hostage;
after seven days of negotiation,

126
00:08:30,713 --> 00:08:34,025
conducted for Singapore
by a team of officials led

127
00:08:34,105 --> 00:08:37,756
by S. R. Nathan, the hijackers
released their remaining

128
00:08:37,836 --> 00:08:41,975
hostages and were flown out to
Kuwait on the 8th of February.

129
00:08:41,975 --> 00:08:44,550
The island went back to work.

130
00:08:44,550 --> 00:08:47,845
The modern plant has
had two darker chapters,

131
00:08:47,925 --> 00:08:49,875
both on the public record.

132
00:08:49,875 --> 00:08:53,746
On the 28th of September
2011, fire broke out in a

133
00:08:53,826 --> 00:08:56,828
processing area of pumps and pipelines;

134
00:08:56,908 --> 00:09:01,650
Shell evacuated its staff and
shut down the entire refinery.

135
00:09:01,650 --> 00:09:05,274
The fire took more than
thirty hours to extinguish,

136
00:09:05,354 --> 00:09:08,760
fought by the SCDF and
Shell's own firefighters,

137
00:09:08,840 --> 00:09:13,125
and the plant was back to full
production only in December.

138
00:09:13,125 --> 00:09:16,700
The second chapter ran quietly for years.

139
00:09:16,700 --> 00:09:21,392
From at least 2014, rogue
employees siphoned marine gas oil

140
00:09:21,472 --> 00:09:26,244
from the plant and sold it at
sea; Shell noticed unexplained

141
00:09:26,324 --> 00:09:31,500
losses in 2015, and in January
2018 seventeen men were arrested.

142
00:09:31,500 --> 00:09:35,367
In court, the scheme was
put at more than 350,000

143
00:09:35,447 --> 00:09:40,281
tonnes of gas oil, worth over
200 million Singapore dollars —

144
00:09:40,361 --> 00:09:43,825
the largest theft of
its kind in Singapore.

145
00:09:43,825 --> 00:09:49,896
The first mastermind, Juandi Pungot, was
jailed for twenty-nine years in 2022;

146
00:09:49,976 --> 00:09:54,864
the second, Abdul Latif Ibrahim,
for twenty-five years in 2025,

147
00:09:54,944 --> 00:09:59,675
with other former employees
drawing long terms between them.

148
00:09:59,675 --> 00:10:03,825
In May 2010 the site had taken
its modern shape with the

149
00:10:03,905 --> 00:10:07,828
opening of the Shell Eastern
Petrochemicals Complex —

150
00:10:07,908 --> 00:10:12,663
a 4.1 billion Singapore dollars
project built around an ethylene

151
00:10:12,743 --> 00:10:17,275
cracker on Bukom, tied by subsea
pipelines to Jurong Island.

152
00:10:17,275 --> 00:10:22,170
But in May 2024 Shell announced
the sale of the whole Singapore

153
00:10:22,250 --> 00:10:26,356
energy and chemicals park,
Bukom and Jurong together,

154
00:10:26,436 --> 00:10:31,174
and on the 1st of April 2025
the sale was completed to CAPGC,

155
00:10:31,254 --> 00:10:36,150
a joint venture between Indonesia's
Chandra Asri and Glencore.

156
00:10:36,150 --> 00:10:40,833
The island now trades as the
Aster Energy and Chemicals Park —

157
00:10:40,913 --> 00:10:46,057
a refinery of 237,000 barrels a
day, on the sale documents' figures,

158
00:10:46,137 --> 00:10:49,360
with its 1.1
million-tonne-a-year cracker —

159
00:10:49,440 --> 00:10:53,739
and in September 2026 its new
owners launched a terminals

160
00:10:53,819 --> 00:10:58,275
business around Bukom's thirteen
wharves and tank storage.

161
00:10:58,275 --> 00:11:02,625
The work never stopped; only
the name over the gate changed.

162
00:11:02,625 --> 00:11:05,675
Even the ferry has been renewed.

163
00:11:05,675 --> 00:11:09,408
Since 2023 the crossing
from Pasir Panjang has been

164
00:11:09,488 --> 00:11:13,146
served by fully electric
ferries — the first fully

165
00:11:13,226 --> 00:11:18,604
electric ferry service in Singapore
— beginning with the Penguin Refresh,

166
00:11:18,684 --> 00:11:21,450
unveiled at Bukom in April that year.

167
00:11:21,450 --> 00:11:25,036
One cloud belongs in the
record, stated carefully:

168
00:11:25,116 --> 00:11:29,582
two oil leaks at the site in
2024 led to Shell Singapore being

169
00:11:29,662 --> 00:11:33,908
charged in September 2026 in
connection with the discharges

170
00:11:33,988 --> 00:11:37,647
and their reporting; the
case is before the courts,

171
00:11:37,727 --> 00:11:42,200
and concerns the period when
Shell still operated the island.

172
00:11:42,200 --> 00:11:45,400
Bukom's story may not be finished.

173
00:11:45,400 --> 00:11:49,233
At the National Day Rally in
August 2026, the Prime Minister

174
00:11:49,313 --> 00:11:53,538
announced a long-term plan to merge
Pulau Semakau, Pulau Bukom and

175
00:11:53,618 --> 00:11:58,039
Pulau Sudong into a new "Western
Island", for advanced manufacturing,

176
00:11:58,119 --> 00:12:01,626
power generation and defence,
linked to Jurong Island —

177
00:12:01,706 --> 00:12:05,865
a plan measured in decades, still
under study, with marine groups

178
00:12:05,945 --> 00:12:10,250
raising attributed concerns for
the reefs of the southern islands.

179
00:12:10,250 --> 00:12:14,986
If it happens, the kerosene island
will be reinvented once more,

180
00:12:15,066 --> 00:12:20,178
as it has been roughly once a
generation since 1892: depot, refinery,

181
00:12:20,258 --> 00:12:25,375
petrochemical park, and now, perhaps,
one shore of something larger.

182
00:12:25,375 --> 00:12:28,604
For now it stays what it
has been for a century —

183
00:12:28,684 --> 00:12:32,791
a place most people know only
as a lit shore across the water,

184
00:12:32,871 --> 00:12:37,125
and a few thousand know as the
far end of a ferry ride to work.

185
00:12:37,125 --> 00:12:41,731
Where it fits in the bigger
story: Singapore's oil age did not

186
00:12:41,811 --> 00:12:46,875
begin with Jurong Island or with
the great refineries of the 1970s.

187
00:12:46,875 --> 00:12:51,745
It began in 1892, on a freshwater
island five kilometres offshore,

188
00:12:51,825 --> 00:12:54,595
with tanks of Russian kerosene for the

189
00:12:54,675 --> 00:12:58,945
region's lamps — and the same
island then hosted the town,

190
00:12:59,025 --> 00:13:03,075
the refinery and the
petrochemical park that followed.

191
00:13:03,075 --> 00:13:08,025
Bukom is the industry's first
address, and it has never closed.
